## padLeft(` section of README.md: it names the arguments (value, width, pad character) and says it returns the value left-padded to width with that character, unchanged when already that long."},{"line":"`npm test` exits 0 and the test files include at least one test that calls padLeft","proof":"run","how":"npm test; echo exit=$?; grep -l padLeft test/*.js"}],"non_goals":["Right padding, centring, or other new string helpers beyond padLeft","Changing the behaviour or signatures of capitalize, slugify or truncate","Publishing a release to npm or bumping the package version","Converting README to a different documentation format"],"assumptions":["Signature is padLeft(value, width, char): value may be a string or number (numbers are converted to their decimal string), width is the minimum result length, char is the single pad character; the expect lines always pass char explicitly, so the default when char is omitted is left to the builder (a space, matching String.prototype.padStart, is the suggested default).","When value is already width characters or longer, it is returned unchanged (not truncated).","\"Keep the README's list of functions complete\" means every exported function has a `## name(args)` section like the existing ones; since slugify is exported but undocumented today, it is added too.","Behaviour for negative numbers, multi-character pad strings or non-integer widths is not specified and not checked."]}}